# time-to-play - the two numbers a player feels, measured on one VM guest.
#
#   tools/time-to-play --port 10025 --identity /tmp/rocknix-vm-pair/qa-key \
#       --monitor /tmp/rocknix-qemu-monitor-c.sock --backend sftp \
#       --repeat 5 --out /tmp/ttp
#
# WHY. Time to play is a first-class metric (D-CLOUD-098, #135): how long
# from the interface to a game's first frame, and from one game's exit to
# the next game's first frame. Budgets for both are register rows, and a row
# is only ever set from a measurement (D-CLOUD-116) -- so something has to
# take the measurement, on the VM and never on a console (vm-first.md).
# This is that something.
#
# WHAT IT MEASURES, and how each number is defined:
#
#   launch    UI to game, at rest. From the POST that EmulationStation's own
#             HTTP API turns into ViewController::launch -- the same code
#             path as pressing A on the game -- to the first frame the game
#             draws. "First frame" is the first screendump that differs from
#             the one before it AT OR AFTER the RetroArch process exists;
#             the screen leaves the carousel earlier than that (the interface
#             tears its window down first), which is why the two are
#             separate numbers here and the earlier one is not the answer.
#             Corroborated by RetroArch's CPU ticks: the exit cell's rule is
#             that RetroArch is drawing once utime+stime reach 200
#             (awk '{print $14+$15}' /proc/<pid>/stat).
#
#   exit      The exit sync alone. From the shipped execute_kill (extracted
#             from the installed input_sense, so the signal is the one under
#             test) to (a) the last-sync-exit stamp being written -- the work
#             ending -- and (b) the card leaving the screen, which is later
#             by the linger the card holds its outcome for. Both are
#             reported: the stamp is when the saves are safe, the card is
#             when the player stops being told something is happening.
#
#   g2g       Game to game. The same exit, with a launch fired the moment the
#             emulator is gone. What the launch does to the sync is read off
#             the stamp's token afterwards -- `cancelled` is D-CLOUD-076's
#             behaviour today, `completed` would mean it ran to the end --
#             so the cell reports what the build does rather than assuming.
#
#   startup   The startup sync: cloudsaves.startup=1, reboot, and time from
#             the reboot to the last-sync-startup stamp; then a launch fired
#             while the startup sync is still running. The setting is put
#             back afterwards.
#
#   offline   D-CLOUD-112's benchmark: the same exit sync with no
#             connectivity, four ways -- a refused port, no default route, a
#             blackholed route to the endpoint, and a captive portal
#             answering HTTP for everything -- each beside the online number,
#             because the claim under test is that the offline exit reaches
#             its outcome in about the time a successful one would have
#             taken.
#
#   bounds    D-CLOUD-111's three bounds as they stand TODAY: the refused
#             port, an endpoint that accepts and never answers, and the
#             captive portal. How long until the sync ends, and with what
#             outcome on the card. Frames every half second are kept.
#
# The payload. D-CLOUD-116 asks for a game exited with an auto-state plus a
# couple of manual save states and their screenshots. Probe.nes through
# RetroArch on the guest leaves a 514-byte auto-state and a 506-byte
# thumbnail -- a payload so small that it measures the round trip and
# nothing else -- so the cell seeds realistic files instead and says so in
# its report: three states of ~180 KiB, three screenshots of ~18 KiB and an
# 8 KiB battery save, sizes jittered per repeat so that a backend comparing
# by size alone (WebDAV with vendor=other) still sees a change. --payload srm
# seeds only the battery save, which is P-13's "one changed battery save".
#
# The AUTO-state in what actually moves is RetroArch's own, not the seeded
# one: runemu.sh launches with -autosave 1, so RetroArch rewrites
# Probe.state.auto and its thumbnail on the way out and the seeded pair is
# replaced before the sync reads them. So a measured exit moves seven files
# and about 440 KiB -- a real auto-state and its screenshot, two synthetic
# manual states with theirs, and the battery save -- which is D-CLOUD-116's
# shape with the one file that could be real being real. The cell reports
# what it seeded (`du`); the run's rclone line in the log reports what moved.
#
# WHAT IT DOES TO THE GUEST, and puts back: the rclone remote's address (for
# the offline and bounds cells), cloudsaves.startup (for the startup cell),
# the guest's default route (for one offline cell), and the seeded saves.
# Nothing else. A copy of rclone.conf is taken before the first change and
# restored on every exit, including a signal.
#
# TIMING RESOLUTION. A screendump round trip through the QEMU monitor costs
# about 0.2 s on a 640x480 guest, so every frame-derived number is quoted to
# that. The SSH round trip (a ControlMaster is held open) is measured at the
# start of the run and reported beside the numbers, because it sits inside
# the launch number: the POST goes over it.
